// 六级 8000 词 · 每组配套短文 freq.basic（由 build/passages/freq.basic.txt 生成）
CET6.passages("freq.basic",{"1":{"w":["the","be","to","of","a","and","in","it","that","they","have","for","do","their","on","we","with","as","you","what"],"title":"The Words We Use Most","t":"Linguists who count words in newspapers and exams always reach **the** same conclusion: a few short words do most of **the** work in English. They may **be** small, but without them no sentence could stand.\n\nConsider a simple sentence: \"People who move **to** a new city often feel lonely at first.\" Most **of** the meaning comes from the longer words, yet it is **a** handful of tiny ones that hold the structure together, like nails **and** screws in a wooden chair. Put them **in** the wrong place, and **it** falls apart.\n\nTeachers know **that** learners often ignore these words. **They** focus on difficult vocabulary and **have** little patience **for** grammar. But what learners **do** with small words often decides **their** scores. A student who writes \"depend **on**\" correctly, or knows when to use \"a\" instead of \"the\", sounds natural; one who doesn't sounds foreign.\n\nSo what should **we** do? Read widely, and pay attention to the words that appear **with** every noun and verb. Treat them **as** old friends rather than background noise. If **you** notice how native writers use them, you will soon find that **what** once seemed difficult becomes easy."},"2":{"w":["can","more","people","from","but","by","this","about","or","not","at","one","make","say","will","when","than","other","your","our"],"title":"Why Small Choices Matter","t":"Psychologists say that **people** often underestimate the power of small daily choices. We believe that our lives are shaped **by** big decisions — where to study, whom to marry, which job to accept. **But** research suggests that habits matter just as much.\n\nThink **about** **this** example. A student who reads for twenty minutes every evening will read **more** than thirty books in a year. Another student, who waits for a free weekend, may read only two **or** three. The difference does **not** come **from** intelligence; it comes from routine.\n\nThe same is true **at** work. Employees who **make** a short plan each morning finish tasks faster **than** those who simply react to emails. \"Most people **say** they lack time,\" one researcher explains, \"**when** they really lack a system.\"\n\nOf course, no **one** can change every habit at once. Experts suggest choosing a single habit and keeping it for a month. Some prefer to start with sleep; **other** people begin with exercise or study. Whatever **your** choice, the key is consistency. As **our** grandparents used to say, a long journey begins with one step, and you **can** take that step today. Small changes **will** add up."},"3":{"w":["how","he","there","who","all","work","time","if","so","many","new","use","most","some","study","year","find","child","change","which"],"title":"Learning at Any Age","t":"**How** old is too old to learn something **new**? Many adults believe that learning belongs to childhood, but a recent **study** suggests otherwise. Researchers followed **some** two thousand adults for ten **years|year** and found that those who kept learning stayed healthier and happier.\n\nOne participant, a retired engineer named Mr. Wang, began piano lessons at sixty-eight. \"**He** practises every day,\" his wife says, \"and **there** is music in the house again.\" Another, a woman **who** had never used a computer, learned to **use** online banking and video calls to stay in touch with her grandchildren.\n\n**All** the participants agreed that learning takes effort. \"At first, I made **so** **many** mistakes,\" one woman admits. \"**If** I had worried about looking foolish, I would have given up.\" But **most** of them said that the effort was worth it. Learning gave them confidence, friends and a sense of purpose.\n\nThe researchers believe the findings could **change** how society treats older people. Instead of seeing retirement as the end of **work**, we might see it as a **time** for growth. \"Every **child** is curious,\" the lead scientist says. \"Our task is to **find** ways to keep that curiosity alive — **which** is easier than we think.\""},"4":{"w":["get","life","also","take","up","world","its","like","way","those","these","would","should","may","need","think","go","good","his","help"],"title":"A Good Life","t":"What makes a **good** **life**? Philosophers have debated this question for thousands of years, and people around the **world** still **think** about it today. Some believe happiness comes from wealth; others say it comes from love, health or meaningful work.\n\nMy grandfather had his own answer. **His** advice was simple: \"**Help** others, and you will **get** more than you give.\" He grew **up** in a poor village, and he spent **his** life working as a doctor in the countryside. He never became rich, but he was **also** never lonely. **Those** who knew him remember his kindness more than anything else.\n\nI sometimes **take** his advice when I feel lost. Modern life, with **its** constant pressure and endless choices, can make it hard to know which **way** to **go**. We **may** feel that we **need** more money, a better job or a bigger house. But **these** goals rarely bring lasting peace.\n\nPerhaps we **should** measure success differently. Instead of asking how much we own, we could ask how many people we have helped. My grandfather **would** have **liked|like** that question. \"A life is like a candle,\" he used to say. \"Its value is the light it gives.\""},"5":{"w":["out","come","see","even","food","only","into","because","no","woman","such","thing","know","give","over","her","she","show","student","my"],"title":"The Student Who Cooked","t":"When I moved **into** my first university dormitory, I did not **know** how to cook. At home, my mother had always prepared every meal, and I had **only** ever boiled an egg. **Even** making tea felt like an adventure.\n\nThings changed **because** of a **woman** named Mrs. Liu, who ran a small restaurant near the campus. One evening, she noticed me eating instant noodles for the third time that week. \"**No** **student** should live like this,\" **she** said firmly. She invited me to **come** to her kitchen on Saturday mornings and promised to **show** me a few simple dishes.\n\nI was nervous at first, but **her** patience made learning easy. She taught me that cooking is not about **such** complicated skills as chefs **see** on television. It is about care. \"The most important **thing**,\" she told me, \"is to taste your food and pay attention.\"\n\nOver the next year, I learned to make soup, fried rice and dumplings. **My** friends began to visit my room for dinner, and **food** became a way of making friends. When I graduated, I went back to thank Mrs. Liu. I wanted to **give** her a gift, but she just laughed. \"Cook for someone else,\" she said. \"That is the best way to pay me back.\" My student days are long **over**, but whenever I run **out** of ideas in the kitchen, I still hear her voice."},"6":{"w":["long","become","why","first","well","different","just","could","high","much","learn","often","between","great","better","problem","try","now","feel","job"],"title":"My First Real Job","t":"**Why** do young people find their **first** **job** so stressful? When I started work at a small design company, I thought I was well prepared. I had studied hard at university and done **well** in my exams. But I soon discovered that working life was **different** from anything I had imagined.\n\nOn my first day, my manager gave me a **problem** to solve: a client wanted a new logo by Friday. I **could** not sleep for two nights. I was afraid that my work would not be good enough and that everyone would **feel** disappointed in me. The pressure was **much** **higher|high** than at school.\n\nA senior colleague noticed how nervous I was. \"Don't **try** to be perfect,\" she told me. \"**Just** do your best and ask questions.\" It was **great** advice. She explained that mistakes are a normal part of how we **learn**, and that the gap **between** students and professionals is mostly experience.\n\nOver the next few months, I **became|become** more confident. I learned to plan my time **better**, and I **often** asked for feedback. **Now**, three years later, I lead my own small team. It has been a **long** journey, and I still remember that first frightening week. Whenever a new colleague joins us, I pass on the same advice I was given."},"7":{"w":["day","country","company","look","own","while","two","want","suggest","place","any","important","idea","after","health","through","city","man","without","american"],"title":"Moving to the City","t":"**Two** years ago, my cousin left her small village and moved to a big **city** to work for a technology **company**. Like many young people, she **wanted|want** a better life. \"In my village, there are few jobs,\" she explained. \"The city seemed like the right **place** to build my **own** future.\"\n\nThe first months were hard. Every **day** she spent hours on crowded buses, and she missed the quiet of the **country**. She had no friends and often ate dinner alone. Her **health** suffered, and she began to doubt her decision. She would **look** at photos of home and wonder whether she should go back.\n\nThen a friendly older **man** in her office gave her some advice. He **suggested|suggest** that she join a weekend hiking club. \"You can't get **through** hard times **without** friends,\" he said. It was an **important** lesson. **After** a few weekends in the mountains, she had made several friends, including an **American** engineer who was also new to the city.\n\nToday, my cousin is much happier. She has learned that **any** big change takes time, and **while** city life can be lonely, it also offers many opportunities. Her best **idea**, she says, was to stop waiting for happiness and start looking for it."},"8":{"w":["grow","where","parent","music","then","same","book","too","whether","very","something","school","both","person","around","believe","right","number","young","start"],"title":"The Music Teacher","t":"Every town has **something** special, and in our small town it was Mr. Gao, the music teacher at the primary **school**. He was a **young** man when he arrived, full of energy and ideas. He **believed|believe** that **music** could help children **grow** in confidence, **whether** or not they had any talent.\n\nAt first, some **parents|parent** were doubtful. They thought music was less important than maths or science, and they did not want their children to waste time. Mr. Gao listened politely, **then** invited them to a concert. When they heard their children sing together, **both** mothers and fathers were moved to tears. **Very** few of them complained after that.\n\nMr. Gao treated every child as a unique **person**. He gave each pupil a **book** of songs and encouraged them to write their own. Children who were shy in other classes would often **start** singing loudly in his room. A large **number** of his former students still play instruments today.\n\nHe taught at the school for thirty years. When he retired, students from **around** the country came back to thank him. Many of them said the **same** thing: he made them feel that they had a **right** to dream. \"Music doesn't care where you come from,\" he used to say. \"It only cares **where** you are going.\" And so we sang, **too**."},"9":{"w":["develop","keep","science","always","example","today","every","large","money","eat","each","hard","language","before","few","old","really","another","end","worker"],"title":"Learning a Language Through Food","t":"**Before** I moved to Italy, I studied Italian for a year but **really** understood very little. My teacher gave me **another** piece of advice: \"Learn the **language** through food.\" It sounded strange, but it turned out to be the best tip I received.\n\n**Each** morning in Florence, I went to a **large** market near my flat. The **old** woman at the vegetable stall was patient with me **every** time I visited. She would hold up a tomato or an onion and say its name slowly, and I would repeat it. Within a **few** weeks, I could buy everything I needed to cook and **eat** at home.\n\nLanguage and food are connected in many ways. Researchers in cognitive **science** say that we remember words better when they are linked to strong experiences, such as taste and smell. Cooking also helps learners **develop** practical vocabulary for **today**, not just for exams. And it saves **money**: eating at home is much cheaper than eating out!\n\nOf course, learning was **hard** at times. One **example** I'll never forget: I asked a **worker** at the bakery for \"a kiss\" instead of \"a roll.\" Everyone laughed, including me. But I **always** **kept|keep** trying. By the **end** of the year, I could talk about almost anything — as long as it was about food."},"10":{"w":["age","information","still","college","set","small","lot","themselves","understand","seem","last","pay","reason","back","development","animal","home","tell","family","put"],"title":"The Family Farm","t":"My grandparents have run the same small farm in the hills for fifty years. They grow vegetables, keep chickens and raise a few pigs. At their **age**, most people have retired, but they **still** work in the fields every morning. \"Work keeps us young,\" my grandmother likes to **tell** me.\n\nWhen I was a child, I spent every summer at their **home**. My grandfather taught me to care for each **animal** and to understand the seasons. I remember how he would **put** seeds into the soil with great care, as if each one were a treasure. \"Every plant has a **reason** to grow,\" he would say.\n\nOur **family** has changed a lot since then. Most of my cousins went to **college** in the city and now work in offices. They **seem** to have little interest in farming. My grandparents **understand** this, but they hope that someone will come **back** to take over the farm.\n\nRecent **development** in the region has brought new roads and more **information** about modern farming. Young farmers can now **set** up online shops and sell directly to customers, and they often earn more than office workers who **pay** high rents in the city. Last summer, two of my cousins spent a **lot** of time on the farm. They taught **themselves** how to sell vegetables online, and their first **small** business is already making money. My grandmother says it was the best summer since the **last** time the whole family lived on the farm."},"11":{"w":["talk","face","manager","spend","question","able","big","live","meet","future","down","never","instead","since","week","group","off","past","someone","far"],"title":"A Lesson from My Manager","t":"Last year, our team got a new **manager**, a quiet woman in her fifties. In her first **week**, she called a **group** meeting and asked each of us one simple **question**: \"What do you want your **future** to look like?\" Nobody had ever asked us that before.\n\nSome of us didn't know how to answer. I said I wanted to **live** in a **big** city and travel **far** from home. **Someone** else said he just wanted to stop working late. Our manager listened carefully and wrote **down** every answer.\n\n**Instead** of giving orders, she spent the next month talking with each of us. She would **talk** to us about our strengths and the problems we **faced|face**. \"You will **never** grow if you only do what you did in the **past**,\" she told me. She encouraged me to **spend** an hour a day learning new skills.\n\n**Since** then, our team has changed. We **meet** every Monday to share ideas, and people are more **able** to say what they think. Last month, the manager took a week **off** for the first time, and the team ran smoothly without her. \"That,\" she said when she returned, \"is the best sign of a good team.\""},"12":{"w":["tree","among","mind","call","during","begin","passage","service","ask","write","kind","early","move","must","poor","report","possible","bring","land","little"],"title":"The Old Tree","t":"In the **early** days of our village, people say, a traveller planted a **tree** beside the river. Two hundred years later, it still stands **among** the houses, and the villagers **call** it \"Grandfather Tree.\" Its branches are wide enough to shade the whole square.\n\nThe tree has seen many changes. **During** hard times, **poor** families met under it to share food. Children played there, and old men sat in its shade to talk. When a family had to **move** away, they would **bring** a **little** soil from under the tree to their new home.\n\nLast year, a company wanted to buy the **land** and build a shopping centre. The news spread quickly, and a local reporter wrote a **report** about the tree's history. People from nearby towns began to **write** letters and **ask** the government to protect it. \"It's **possible** to have development,\" one letter said, \"without losing our past.\"\n\nIn the end, the company agreed to change its plans. The tree now has a small fence, and the town offers a guided tour as a public **service**. Visitors often stop to read a **passage** from an old poem carved on a stone nearby. One elderly woman told me, \"You **must** understand: this tree is in the **mind** of everyone here. It is a **kind** of family member.\" When the tour **began|begin** this spring, the whole village came to celebrate."},"13":{"w":["stop","turn","across","moon","once","yet","simply","water","ever","under","cost","practice","chinese","friend","house","month","play","clear","care","hold"],"title":"Under the Moon","t":"Every autumn, my family celebrates the Mid-Autumn Festival at my grandparents' **house** in the countryside. It is one of the most important **Chinese** holidays, a time when families gather to admire the full **moon** and share mooncakes.\n\nThis year, I invited a **friend** from Spain to join us. She had never seen the festival before — this was her first **month** in China — and she was not sure what to expect. I explained that the holiday is **simply** about being together. \"It doesn't **cost** much,\" I said. \"It's about **care**, not money.\"\n\nWe arrived just before sunset. My grandmother made us **stop** at the door to wash our hands with cold **water** from the well, an old **practice** she has kept all her life. Then she **held|hold** my friend's hand and led her **across** the courtyard to a table covered with fruit and cakes.\n\nAs the moon rose, the children began to **play** games, and the adults sat **under** the old pear tree, talking quietly. When the sky became **clear**, my grandfather told the story of Chang'e, the goddess who flew to the moon. My friend listened closely. \"Have you **ever** heard it before?\" I asked. \"No,\" she said, \"but I feel as if I've known it **once**, long ago.\" As we **turned|turn** to go inside, the moon was still shining, though the night was not **yet** over."},"14":{"w":["though","leave","air","drive","next","side","three","dog","already","expect","love","strong","away","case","hour","run","teacher","close","let","here"],"title":"The Dog Who Waited","t":"My **teacher** in primary school had a small brown **dog** named Lucky. Every morning, Lucky walked with her to school and waited by the gate until classes finished. The children **loved|love** him, and he seemed to **love** them too.\n\nOne winter, our teacher fell ill and had to spend **three** weeks in hospital. We **expected|expect** Lucky to stay at home, but he came to the school gate every morning anyway. He sat by the **side** of the road for hours, waiting. Even on the coldest days, when the **air** was freezing, he did not **leave**.\n\nThe headmaster finally decided to **let** him wait inside the classroom. \"In this **case**,\" he said, \"we'll make an exception.\" Lucky lay **close** to the heater, and the children shared their lunch with him. **Though** he was small, he was **strong** and patient.\n\nWhen our teacher came back, Lucky heard her car from far **away**. He jumped up and **ran|run** to the gate, barking with joy. She had **already** heard about his loyalty from the other teachers, and she cried as she hugged him. \"I'm **here**,\" she whispered. **Next** spring, she started to **drive** a shorter route so that Lucky could walk with her for an **hour** every morning."},"15":{"w":["light","enough","healthy","plan","travel","almost","easy","computer","itself","full","build","read","key","everyone","story","yourself","watch","foreign","library","low"],"title":"The New Library","t":"Last year, our town opened a new public **library** in an old factory building. The architects wanted to **build** something bright and welcoming, so they added large windows to let in natural **light**. On sunny days, the reading room is **full** of sunshine.\n\nThe library has become a meeting place for **everyone**. Children come to **read** picture books and listen to a **story** in the afternoon. Students use the **computer** room to study, and older people **watch** films on Saturday evenings. There is even a corner with books in **foreign** languages for people who love to **travel**.\n\nThe best thing about the library is that it is **easy** to use. Membership is free, and the opening hours are long. \"We **plan** to stay open **almost** every day of the year,\" the director told me. She believes that reading is as important as eating **healthy** food: it keeps the mind strong.\n\nOf course, the library is not perfect. It does not have **enough** seats at weekends, and the coffee shop is too small. But the building **itself** is beautiful, and the town's **low** budget did not stop the team from doing a great job. The **key**, the director says, is simple: \"Come in, find a quiet corner and just be **yourself**.\""},"16":{"w":["word","thinking","order","doctor","buy","fail","sound","break","present","together","mean","enjoy","car","tourist","clean","building","field","form","matter","free"],"title":"The Doctor's Advice","t":"My uncle is a **doctor** in a busy city hospital. Last summer, he told me something I have never forgotten: \"Health is not only a **matter** of medicine. It is a **form** of daily care.\" He believes that many people **fail** to look after themselves until it is too late.\n\nHis advice sounds simple. Walk instead of driving the **car**. **Enjoy** meals **together** with family rather than eating alone. Keep your home **clean** and open the windows. Take a short **break** every hour when you work at a computer. \"None of these things costs money,\" he says. \"They are **free**.\"\n\nHe also talks about the importance of **thinking** positively. \"When patients are worried, every small **sound** in their body seems serious,\" he explains. \"I try to calm them with a few kind **words|word**.\" He says that a good doctor must be **present** for patients, not just for their illnesses.\n\nMy uncle works in a modern **building**, but he spends his weekends in the countryside, walking through rice **fields|field** with his camera. He is not a typical **tourist**; he talks to farmers and learns about their lives. \"Our health depends on **order** and balance,\" he says. \"That doesn't **mean** we must be perfect. It means we must pay attention.\" He never tries to **buy** happiness; he finds it in simple things."},"17":{"w":["meeting","bad","plant","market","remember","class","fast","similar","sometimes","raise","short","teach","rich","until","conversation","exercise","half","quite","true","usually"],"title":"The Conversation Class","t":"Last term, I joined an English **conversation** **class** at a community centre. I had studied grammar for years, but I could not hold a **short** conversation without feeling nervous. The teacher, a retired journalist, promised that our speaking would improve **quite** quickly if we practised every week.\n\nEach **meeting** began with a simple **exercise**. We worked in pairs and talked about our daily lives: what we ate, where we shopped at the **market**, how we spent our weekends. **Sometimes** the topics were serious, such as whether **rich** countries should do more to help poor ones. The teacher would **raise** a question and let us argue.\n\nShe had a gift for making people relax. \"Don't worry about **bad** grammar,\" she said. \"Speak first, correct later.\" She liked to **teach** through stories, and she would **usually** tell us one at the start of class. I still **remember** her story about a **plant** that grew through a crack in the pavement. \"Learning is **similar**,\" she said. \"It finds a way.\"\n\nBy the end of the term, I could speak **fast** enough to keep up with a real conversation. It was not easy; for the first **half** of the course I almost gave up. But I stayed **until** the last lesson, and I'm glad I did. It is **true** what she told us: confidence comes from practice."},"18":{"w":["agree","four","open","stay","workplace","news","sell","serve","second","fall","wrong","meaning","modern","farm","million","difficult","note","ago","ourselves","paper"],"title":"From Farm to Office","t":"**Four** years **ago**, my friend Lei left his family's **farm** to work in the city. Like **millions|million** of young people, he hoped to find a **modern** job with a good salary. He found work in an office, but he soon discovered that his new **workplace** was **difficult** in ways he had not expected.\n\nAt first, he felt like a stranger. Colleagues used words whose **meaning** he did not understand, and he made many **wrong** decisions. He took **notes|note** on everything, writing in a small **paper** notebook he kept in his pocket. Every evening, he read the **news** to learn about the business world.\n\nIn his **second** year, things began to change. Lei realised that his farming background was useful. He knew how to **sell** vegetables at the market, and he understood how to **serve** customers patiently. His manager began to **agree** with his ideas, and he was promoted.\n\nStill, Lei misses the countryside, especially in autumn when the leaves **fall**. He goes home every holiday and **stays|stay** for a week. \"We must not forget where we come from,\" he says. \"We should remind **ourselves** that every job has value. An office is not better than a farm. It is just **open** at different hours.\""},"19":{"w":["sure","road","name","simple","thank","body","hope","cut","several","yes","decide","price","subject","works","behind","hear","five","left","nothing","slow"],"title":"The Slow Road","t":"Last spring, I **decided|decide** to walk from my hometown to the next city, a journey of about **five** days. Friends thought I was crazy. \"Why not take the train?\" they asked. I wasn't **sure** myself, but I **hoped|hope** that a **slow** journey would help me think.\n\nThe **road** was quiet and **simple**. At one crossroads, I took a **left** turn just to follow a river. I passed villages whose **names|name** I had never heard, and I stopped to talk with farmers working in the fields. **Several** times, strangers invited me to eat with them. One old man refused to accept money. \"**Nothing** is more valuable than company,\" he said. I could only **thank** him again and again.\n\nThe walk was hard on my **body**. My feet hurt, and I **cut** my hand while climbing over a fence. On the third day, I almost gave up. But I could **hear** my grandfather's voice: \"When you have walked half the way, the other half is **behind** you.\"\n\nBy the end, I had learned that the **price** of speed is attention. In a car, we miss the small things: the smell of rain, the sound of birds, the art in a farmer's garden. I now visit museums with new eyes, studying the **works** of rural painters. Walking has become my favourite **subject** to write about. When people ask whether I'd do it again, I say **yes** without hesitation."},"20":{"w":["machine","visit","answer","bit","bridge","meat","discuss","middle","deep","friendship","receive","along","lie","shoe","hand","soon","carry","per","wide","worth"],"title":"The Old Bridge","t":"In the **middle** of my hometown, a stone **bridge** crosses a **wide** river. It is more than three hundred years old, and people say it was built by **hand** without any **machine**. For centuries, farmers have used it to **carry** vegetables and **meat** to the market on the other side.\n\nWhen I was a boy, my grandfather often took me there. We would walk **along** the river and talk about everything. He told me that the bridge was a symbol of **friendship** between two villages that once fought over water. \"A bridge is **worth** more than a wall,\" he said.\n\nRecently, the town decided to **discuss** whether to replace the old bridge with a modern one. Engineers said that the water was too **deep** and the stone too old. Many residents disagreed. Within a week, the council **received|receive** hundreds of letters asking to keep it.\n\nI went back to **visit** last month. The answer had been decided: the bridge would be repaired, not replaced. Workers will **soon** begin, and they hope to finish in a year. I stood on the bridge for a **bit**, watching children run across in their school **shoes|shoe**. \"The **answer** to the past,\" my grandfather once said, \"should not **lie** in forgetting it.\" The repairs will cost about two hundred yuan **per** person in the town, and almost everyone has paid."},"21":{"w":["push","tv","park","cook","driver","speak","member","sleep","fish","draw","minute","weight","green","check","listen","earth","mother","single","night","happy"],"title":"My Mother's Garden","t":"Every evening after work, my **mother** goes into the small garden behind our house. She says it is the only place where she can **listen** to her own thoughts. While the rest of the family watches **TV**, she waters her vegetables and checks every plant for insects.\n\nThe garden is not large, but it is full of life. There are **green** beans, tomatoes and a tiny pond where a **single** **fish** lives. My mother likes to **check** the soil with her fingers. \"The **earth** tells you what it needs,\" she says. \"You just have to pay attention.\"\n\nShe did not always love gardening. When she was younger, she worked as a taxi **driver** and rarely had time to rest. She would come home late at **night**, too tired to **cook** or even **speak**. Then a doctor warned her that she needed to lose **weight** and **sleep** more. A friend suggested gardening, and she decided to try it.\n\nNow she spends at least thirty **minutes|minute** in the garden every day. She has become a **member** of a local gardening club that meets in the **park** on weekends. Sometimes she brings her sketchbook to **draw** the flowers. \"I never imagined I could be so **happy** with dirt on my hands,\" she laughs. Her advice to anyone who feels stressed is simple: don't **push** yourself too hard; grow something instead."},"22":{"w":["fire","tourism","everything","eye","outside","room","wish","office","again","anything","seven","cover","heart","stand","else","pleasure","western","spring","forget","television"],"title":"A Room with a View","t":"When my grandparents retired, they moved to a small flat in a quiet mountain town. Its best feature is a sunny **room** with a large window. From there, they can see green hills, a river and, in **spring**, fields covered with flowers. \"It is a **pleasure** to wake up here,\" my grandmother says.\n\nThe town has changed in recent years. **Tourism** has brought new hotels and cafés, and visitors from Beijing and Shanghai arrive every weekend. Some local people **wish** the town could stay as it was, but others welcome the money. My grandfather, who once worked in a government **office**, has opinions about **everything** and is happy to share them with anyone.\n\nMy grandparents do not own a **television**. Instead, they spend their evenings **outside**, walking along the river or sitting on a bench. My grandmother says she has a weak **heart**, so she walks slowly. \"The doctor told me I could **stand** for an hour but not run,\" she jokes. \"I have no wish to run anywhere **else**.\"\n\nWhen I visit, we sit by the **fire** in winter or by the window in summer and drink tea. Clouds **cover** the hills, and my grandfather points out birds with a sharp **eye**. He likes to read **Western** novels translated into Chinese, though he admits he **forgets|forget** the characters' names. I always promise to come back **again** soon. I visited **seven** times last year, and I would not change **anything** about those visits."},"23":{"w":["restaurant","late","surprise","drink","fit","necessary","worry","game","perfect","smell","forward","post","third","morning","rest","coffee","copy","sit","bag","sport"],"title":"The Morning Café","t":"On the corner of my street, there is a small **restaurant** that serves the best breakfast in town. Every **morning**, office workers stop there to **drink** a cup of **coffee** and eat fresh bread before work. The owner, Mrs. Fang, knows almost every customer by name.\n\nI discovered the café by **surprise**. One rainy day, I was running **late** for an important meeting and needed somewhere to **sit** down. Mrs. Fang brought me tea and told me not to **worry**. \"Nothing is more **necessary** than breakfast,\" she said. That was the start of a long friendship.\n\nThe café is not **perfect**. The tables are small, and the kitchen is so close that the **smell** of frying onions gets into your clothes. But people love it. On weekends, families come to **rest**, read the newspaper or play a board **game**. Mrs. Fang even keeps a **copy** of every local newspaper for customers.\n\nShe is also a keen fan of **sport**. On the wall, there is a **post** card from a famous football player who once ate there. \"He came on the **third** day after I opened,\" she remembers proudly. She keeps fit by walking to work, carrying a heavy **bag** of fresh vegetables. She looks **forward** to retiring one day, but not yet. \"As long as I'm **fit**, I'll keep cooking,\" she says."},"24":{"w":["size","walk","bear","girl","sugar","red","clothes","homework","famous","moment","north","six","store","beautiful","return","lesson","bottle","piece","dollar","either"],"title":"The Shop on North Street","t":"My aunt runs a small clothing **store** on **North** Street, one of the oldest streets in our town. It is not **famous**, but it is **beautiful**, with wooden floors and large windows. Customers come not only for the **clothes** but also for her friendly conversation.\n\nShe opened the shop **six** years ago, after spending a **moment** of crisis deciding whether to leave her office job. \"I couldn't **bear** another year at a desk,\" she says. She used her savings to buy fabric and sewing machines, and she taught herself how to design dresses for women of every **size**.\n\nRunning a business taught her many **lessons|lesson**. In the first year, she lost money and often wanted to give up. But she learned to listen to customers and **return** their trust with good service. She also learned that she could sell **either** expensive dresses or cheap ones, but not both.\n\nHer shop is also a meeting place for local girls. After school, a **girl** from the next street often comes to do her **homework** at the counter, and my aunt gives her a **piece** of cake and a **bottle** of water. On the wall hangs her first **dollar**, sent by an American customer, framed in **red** paper. My aunt is careful with money; she puts very little **sugar** in her tea and rarely buys anything for herself. \"A good **walk** and a good chat,\" she says, \"are better than any shopping trip.\""},"25":{"w":["expensive","catch","latest","street","bird","hospital","maybe","ship","countryside","british","pass","table","meal","certainly","kitchen","send","agricultural","motivation","yellow","baby"],"title":"Life in the Countryside","t":"After living in the city for ten years, my friend Anna, a **British** teacher, decided to move to the **countryside** in southern China. She rented a small house with a **kitchen** garden and a view of rice fields. \"City life was **expensive** and tiring,\" she explains. \"I wanted something simpler.\"\n\nHer new life is very different. In the morning, she wakes to the sound of a **bird** singing outside her window. She walks down the only **street** in the village to buy vegetables and eggs, and farmers often **pass** her a few extra tomatoes for free. Neighbours invite her to share a **meal** at their **table** at least twice a week.\n\nOf course, there are challenges. The nearest **hospital** is an hour away, and she once had to travel by boat when the road flooded; a small **ship** carried patients across the river. When she **caught|catch** a fever last winter, a neighbour cared for her like a family member. She also struggled to understand the local **agricultural** methods at first.\n\nAnna now teaches English online and **sends|send** short videos to friends back home. Her **motivation**, she says, comes from the people around her. A neighbour recently had a **baby**, and Anna was invited to the celebration, where everyone wore **yellow** for luck. \"I'm **certainly** not rich,\" she says, \"but I'm happy.\" She follows the **latest** news from the city, and she says she doesn't miss it. **Maybe**, she thinks, she will stay forever."},"26":{"w":["shop","double","interesting","blue","holiday","warm","window","agreement","welcome","box","head","nearly","picture","cheap","fruit","heavy","weather","south","text","email"],"title":"A Holiday by the Sea","t":"Last summer, my family spent our **holiday** in a small fishing town in the **south**. The **weather** was **warm**, the sky was **blue**, and the sea was so clear that we could see fish swimming near the shore. It was the most relaxing week I'd had in years.\n\nWe stayed in a **cheap** guesthouse with a **window** facing the harbour. The owner, an old fisherman, gave us a warm **welcome** and a **box** of fresh **fruit** on the first day. He said tourists were becoming more common, but the town still felt like a place where everyone knew each other.\n\nEvery morning, I walked to the market to **shop** for breakfast. The stalls were full of **interesting** things: dried fish, sea shells and **heavy** baskets of crabs. I took a **picture** of almost everything and sent them to friends by **email**. My sister preferred to **text** her friends, sending them **double** the number of photos I did.\n\nOne evening, the owner invited us to join a local festival. Fishermen carried a wooden boat on their **heads|head** through the streets, and children threw flowers into the sea. \"We have an **agreement** with the sea,\" the owner explained. \"We respect it, and it feeds us.\" By the end of the week, we had **nearly** forgotten our busy city lives."},"27":{"w":["river","chef","ready","mistake","star","train","foot","party","bank","running","noise","orange","wear","trouble","date","myself","floor","throw","dish","ahead"],"title":"The Chef's First Party","t":"My cousin is a young **chef** who works in a busy restaurant beside the **river** in our city. Last month, she was asked to cook for a large **party**: a **bank** was celebrating its fiftieth anniversary, and two hundred guests were expected. She had never cooked for so many people before.\n\nThe preparation took days. She planned every **dish** carefully, choosing a menu of local fish, vegetables and a dessert made with **orange** and honey. She checked the **date** three times to make sure everything would be **ready**. \"I didn't want to make a single **mistake**,\" she said.\n\nOn the day of the party, things went wrong. The kitchen was full of **noise**, a pot of soup fell on the **floor**, and an assistant arrived late because his **train** was delayed. My cousin had to keep **running** between the kitchen and the hall. She was in **trouble**, but she did not panic.\n\nLater she told me that she had one rule: stay calm and think **ahead**. \"I kept telling **myself** not to panic,\" she said. She asked a colleague to **throw** away the spilled soup and make a new one, and she put her best **foot** forward. When the meal was finally served, the guests applauded. That night, she felt like a **star**. She still likes to **wear** the apron from that day as a lucky charm."},"28":{"w":["friendly","medicine","miss","autonomous","sun","whom","film","mine","disability","fill","busy","suggestion","wait","west","bed","village","traveler","please","above","die"],"title":"The Village Doctor","t":"In a small mountain **village** in the **west** of China, a young doctor named Dr. Lin runs the only clinic. She is **friendly** and patient, and the villagers trust her completely. She treats everyone, including farmers, children and a few elderly people with a **disability**.\n\nHer days are **busy**. She starts early in the morning and often works until late at night. Patients sometimes **wait** for hours outside the clinic, sitting in the **sun**. She has only one assistant and limited **medicine**, so she must use her resources carefully. In winter, she sometimes sleeps on a small **bed** in the back room.\n\nDr. Lin came from the city. Her parents wanted her to work in a large hospital, but she made her own choice. \"The city has many doctors,\" she says. \"Here, people really need me.\" The village has been an **autonomous** community for centuries, and its people are proud and independent.\n\nA **traveler** who visited last year wrote a report about her work and made a short **film**. After it was shown online, people sent donations to **fill** the clinic with new equipment. One viewer's **suggestion** was to build a small library, and Dr. Lin loved the idea. She says she **misses|miss** her family, but she has found a home **above** the clouds. \"This life is **mine**,\" she says. \"**Please** don't feel sorry for me.\" The old village chief, for **whom** she cared until he **died|die** last spring, called her \"our mountain daughter.\""},"29":{"w":["worried","cool","himself","math","mountain","bright","door","everywhere","fine","finish","photo","grade","lunch","join","below","ground","jump","near","whose","deeply"],"title":"A Day in the Mountains","t":"Last Saturday, my friend Hao and I climbed a **mountain** near our city. Hao was **worried** about the weather, but the morning was **bright** and **cool**, perfect for hiking. We set off early and planned to **finish** the climb before **lunch**.\n\nThe path was steep, and in some places we had to **jump** over rocks. Hao, who teaches **math** at a high school, kept counting our steps. \"We've done two thousand already,\" he said. \"**Fine**,\" I replied, \"but how many more?\" Wild flowers grew **everywhere**, and birds sang in the trees **below** us.\n\nNear the top, we met an old man sitting on the **ground**, eating noodles. He told us he climbed the mountain every week, even at seventy-five. He had once been a soldier, and he said he still pushed **himself** to stay strong. \"**Whose** idea was it to climb today?\" he asked. Hao laughed and pointed at me.\n\nAt the summit, we took a **photo** together and looked down at the city. It seemed so small from **near** the clouds. Hao said he wanted to **join** a hiking club, and I agreed to come with him. The old man walked us to the **door** of a small temple on the way down. I felt **deeply** grateful for such a peaceful day. Our legs ached, but our **grade** for the day, Hao joked, was an A."},"30":{"w":["english","father","hi","ill","station","workforce","arrive","credit","east","guest","page","summer","rain","season","wildlife","guess","mention","hotel","march","herself"],"title":"A Summer in the Hills","t":"Last **summer**, my **father** and I spent two weeks at a small **hotel** in the hills of Yunnan. It was the rainy **season**, and it seemed to **rain** almost every afternoon. But the countryside was green and beautiful, and we didn't mind.\n\nWe **arrived|arrive** by bus at a tiny **station** at the bottom of the hill. The owner of the hotel, a cheerful woman, met us with a big smile. \"**Hi**! Welcome!\" she said in **English**, the only English word she seemed to know besides \"OK.\" She treated every **guest** like family.\n\nThe area is famous for its **wildlife**. Every morning, we walked into the forest to watch birds and monkeys. My father, who had been **ill** the year before, grew stronger every day. He kept a notebook and filled a **page** with notes after each walk. \"**Guess** how many birds I saw today,\" he would ask me at dinner.\n\nThe owner ran the hotel by **herself**. She told us that young people were leaving the village, and the local **workforce** was getting older. Tourism was helping, she said, but she wished the government would give small businesses more **credit**. She **mentioned|mention** that she planned to open a small café next **March**, facing the **east**, where the sunrise is beautiful."},"31":{"w":["round","somewhere","ten","wife","excuse","hill","educator","beef","town","eight","front","letter","seat","boom","classroom","map","oh","pair","unhealthy","boy"],"title":"The Teacher in the Hills","t":"Mr. Zhou has been an **educator** for thirty years, teaching in a small school on a **hill** above his hometown. When he started, the **town** was poor, and the school had only one **classroom**. Today, thanks to an economic **boom** in the region, there are **ten** classrooms and more than three hundred students.\n\nHe still remembers his first day. **Eight** children sat in the **front** row, staring at him nervously. One **boy** had walked two hours to get there, carrying a **pair** of shoes in his hand so they would not get dirty. \"**Oh**, how small they were,\" Mr. Zhou says with a smile.\n\nOver the years, he has seen many changes. Children used to eat simple meals of rice and vegetables; now some eat too much **unhealthy** food, and he teaches them about nutrition. Instead of a **letter** from relatives in the city, students now receive video calls. He hangs a large **map** of the world on the wall so that they can see how far their families have travelled.\n\nHis **wife**, also a teacher, works at the same school. On weekends, they cook **beef** noodles for students who cannot go home. Mr. Zhou never makes an **excuse** to leave early, and every term he moves the students **round** the room so that each one gets a **seat** near the window. He hopes that, **somewhere** in the future, one of them will return to teach in the hills."},"32":{"w":["cleaner","european","glass","newspaper","sky","trip","menu","juice","match","afraid","chat","crowded","quarter","sea","tomorrow","totally","challenging","monday","none","january"],"title":"A Trip to the Coast","t":"Last **Monday**, my friend and I took a short **trip** to a fishing town by the **sea**. It was **January**, and the beach was empty. The **sky** was grey, the wind was cold, and the water looked **totally** different from the bright summer pictures I had seen in a **newspaper**.\n\nWe had planned to swim, but neither of us dared. \"I'm **afraid** I'll freeze,\" my friend said. Instead, we walked along the shore and watched fishermen bring in their boats. The town was quiet — **none** of the shops were **crowded**, and the restaurant owners were happy to **chat** with us.\n\nAt lunch, we found a small restaurant with a **menu** written on a board. We ordered fried fish, rice and fresh orange **juice**. A **European** couple at the next table told us they had been travelling around China for a month. \"Your country is **challenging** for travellers,\" they said, \"but always interesting.\"\n\nThat afternoon, we watched a local football **match** on a field near the harbour. A **cleaner** from our hotel was playing, and he scored a goal in the last **quarter** of the game. We cheered so loudly that people turned to look at us. Before leaving, we each bought a small **glass** bottle filled with sand. \"Let's come back **tomorrow**,\" my friend joked. We both knew we would return in summer."},"33":{"w":["mom","suffering","bell","fat","surface","borrow","hundred","weekend","afternoon","annoy","fly","harmless","ice","pick","dress","gift","graduation","outgoing","dark","daughter"],"title":"My Sister's Graduation","t":"Last summer, my older sister celebrated her **graduation** from university. It was a hot **afternoon**, and the ceremony took place outside in a big park. My **mom** cried from the moment it started, and even my father, who rarely shows emotion, seemed close to tears.\n\nMy sister is the first **daughter** in our family to go to university. She has always been **outgoing** and energetic, but her four years of study were not easy. She worked at a café every **weekend** to pay her rent, and she sometimes had to **borrow** money from friends. Her **suffering** during the exam months was real; she barely slept.\n\nFor the ceremony, she wore a simple blue **dress** under her graduation gown. When her name was called, a **bell** rang and more than a **hundred** students threw their caps into the air. A small dog ran across the stage, chasing the caps — **harmless**, but very funny.\n\nAfterwards, we gave her a **gift**: a silver bracelet with her name on the **surface**. My mother had chosen it carefully. \"Now you can **fly**,\" she said. We had dinner at a restaurant that evening and ordered too much **fat** and fried food, but nobody cared. My little brother tried to **annoy** my sister by telling embarrassing stories from her childhood. When we walked home after **dark**, she asked us to stop and **pick** some flowers. My father bought ice cream with extra **ice** for everyone."},"34":{"w":["hunter","radio","sweet","basketball","dinner","everybody","nice","arm","black","factory","fourth","garden","quick","straight","vegetable","website","white","adolescence","husband","ring"],"title":"Grandfather's Garden","t":"My grandfather was a **hunter** in his youth, but in old age he became a gardener. Behind his house in the countryside, he grew every **vegetable** you can imagine: cabbages, beans, carrots and **sweet** potatoes. **Everybody** in the village came to ask for his advice.\n\nHe worked in a **factory** for most of his life, and when he retired, the garden became his joy. Every morning, he listened to the **radio** while he watered the plants. He wore an old **black** hat and always carried a basket over one **arm**. The rows in his garden were always **straight**, as if he had used a ruler.\n\nMy grandmother loved flowers more than vegetables, and she often joked that her **husband** cared more about cabbages than about her. She grew **white** roses along the fence, and my grandfather let her have the **fourth** part of the garden for them. They argued about it every spring, but it was always a **nice** kind of argument.\n\nWhen I was in my **adolescence**, I spent many summers there. My cousins and I played **basketball** in the yard and helped pick beans for **dinner**. My grandfather was **quick** to praise us when we worked hard. Last year, my cousin built a **website** to sell vegetables from the farm. My grandfather still wears the wedding **ring** my grandmother gave him fifty years ago, and he still goes to the **garden** every morning."},"35":{"w":["sick","tired","april","dialogue","exam","hearing","ticket","viewer","workday","flight","quiet","soldier","tuesday","wind","beat","pioneer","twice","amazing","freezing","hot"],"title":"The Freezing Flight","t":"Last **April**, I took a **flight** from Beijing to Harbin for a job interview. I was nervous and **tired**, because I had studied for an important **exam** the night before. I had bought the cheapest **ticket** I could find. The plane left on a **Tuesday** morning, and I hoped to get some sleep on board.\n\nBut the weather had other plans. A strong **wind** began to shake the plane, and the pilot announced that we would land in a different city. Passengers became **quiet**, and a baby began to cry. I felt almost **sick** with worry. My heart **beat** so fast that I could hear it.\n\nNext to me sat an elderly **soldier** in uniform. He noticed my fear and started a friendly **dialogue**. He told me about his life as a **pioneer** in the northern forests, building roads in **freezing** temperatures. \"This wind is nothing,\" he laughed. \"I've seen worse **twice** a year.\"\n\nWe landed safely in a small city. It was so cold that the **hot** tea at the airport cooled in minutes. I missed my interview, but the company offered me another chance. A television **viewer** later told me she had seen a news report about our flight. Thanks to the old man, the experience felt **amazing** rather than frightening. His **hearing** was poor, so I had to shout my thanks. I still think of him on every long **workday**, and I hope I'll meet him again."},"36":{"w":["mentally","nine","saying","surrounding","agenda","cry","february","laugh","october","pull","supermarket","bike","breakfast","plate","son","cocoa","mail","silk","tooth","firefighter"],"title":"The Firefighter's Family","t":"My neighbour Mr. Liu is a **firefighter** who has worked at the city fire station for twenty years. His job is dangerous and **mentally** exhausting, but he says he would never choose another career. \"When you save someone's life, you never forget it,\" he told me.\n\nHis day begins early. He eats a simple **breakfast** of rice porridge and an egg, then rides his **bike** to work. His wife packs his lunch on a small **plate** covered with cloth. Their **son**, who is **nine**, often runs after him to the corner, shouting goodbye.\n\nLast **February**, there was a fire in a **supermarket** near our street. Mr. Liu and his team arrived within minutes. Smoke filled the **surrounding** buildings, and people began to **cry** for help. He had to **pull** an elderly woman out of a burning room. She lost a **tooth** when she fell, but she survived. In **October**, she sent him a thank-you card by **mail**, along with a silk scarf.\n\nMr. Liu likes to **laugh**, and he is full of old **sayings|saying**. His favourite is: \"Fire is a good servant but a bad master.\" On his days off, he makes hot **cocoa** for the neighbourhood children and teaches them about fire safety. His wife keeps a family **agenda** on the fridge so that she knows his shifts. She once gave him a **silk** tie for his birthday, though he jokes that he has never worn it."},"37":{"w":["unsafe","abroad","egg","nor","adjustment","block","dig","dry","dynasty","fifth","hello","mineral","thirty","yours","airport","assistance","boring","cloud","gate","lake"],"title":"Waiting at the Airport","t":"Last summer, I spent **thirty** hours at an **airport** in Europe because my flight home was cancelled. It was my first time travelling **abroad** alone, and I was nervous. \"**Hello**,\" said the woman at the information desk, \"I'm afraid there's nothing we can do until tomorrow.\"\n\nAt first, I was angry. The airport was crowded and **boring**, and I couldn't find anywhere comfortable to sleep. My phone battery was dead, and I had neither a charger **nor** enough money for a hotel. I sat near **gate** number seven and watched planes take off into the grey **clouds|cloud**.\n\nThen an older couple sat down next to me. They offered me a bottle of **mineral** water and a boiled **egg**. \"Is this seat **yours**?\" the man asked politely. We started talking, and I learned that they were historians who studied the Tang **Dynasty**. They had visited China five times and were on their **fifth** trip to Europe.\n\nTheir **assistance** made a big difference. They lent me a charger and told me about a park with a **lake** near the airport where I could walk. They also showed me how to **dig** through the airline's website to find a better flight. The weather was **dry** and sunny, and the walk helped me relax. \"Travel is all about **adjustment**,\" the woman said. \"Things go wrong, and you adapt.\" Before we parted, we exchanged addresses. That evening we found a whole **block** of small restaurants near the park and had dinner together. The airport was not **unsafe**, just tiring, and I left with new friends."},"38":{"w":["ride","sorry","spare","tea","tonight","wall","enjoyable","overweight","willingness","coke","trainer","asian","bent","careful","crossing","flower","hungry","neither","personally","sad"],"title":"The Personal Trainer","t":"Last year, my doctor told me I was **overweight** and needed to exercise. I hated sports, but I agreed to see a **trainer** at the gym near my office. \"I'm **sorry**,\" I told him on the first day, \"but I have no **willingness** to run.\"\n\nHe laughed. \"Then we won't run. We'll **ride** bikes and walk.\" He was **careful** not to push me too hard. Every session began with a cup of green **tea** and a short conversation. He **personally** designed a plan for me, based on my **spare** time and my eating habits.\n\nThe first weeks were difficult. I was always **hungry**, and I missed my daily can of **Coke**. Sometimes I felt **sad** and wanted to give up. But my trainer encouraged me. He told me about his own childhood in an **Asian** village, where he walked ten kilometres to school every day. \"Your body is not **bent** on failing,\" he said. \"It just needs time.\"\n\nSlowly, exercise became **enjoyable**. I started walking to work, passing a busy pedestrian **crossing** and a park full of **flowers|flower**. I even painted a motivational quote on my bedroom **wall**. After six months, I had lost fifteen kilograms. **Neither** my doctor nor I could believe it. \"Let's celebrate **tonight**,\" my trainer said. \"But not with cake.\""},"39":{"w":["thousand","apple","disappointment","fun","glad","modem","nobody","polite","publicly","smile","strange","august","chair","facial","founding","kindergarten","nurse","rebuild","sailing","secretary"],"title":"The Kindergarten Nurse","t":"Ms. Bai has worked as a **nurse** at a **kindergarten** for twelve years. She looks after more than a **thousand** children each year, treating cuts, fevers and the occasional broken arm. The children love her **smile**, and parents trust her completely.\n\nShe chose the job by chance. After graduating, she worked as a **secretary** in an office, but she found the work **strange** and lonely. \"**Nobody** talked to me all day,\" she remembers. One **August**, she saw an advertisement for a kindergarten nurse and decided to apply. It was the best decision of her life.\n\nHer days are full of **fun**. Children bring her drawings and **apples|apple**, and she keeps a small **chair** in her office for anyone who needs a rest, next to an old **modem** that no longer works and that the children love to \"repair.\" She teaches them to be **polite** and to wash their hands before meals. She even paints **facial** designs on children's cheeks during festivals.\n\nThere have been moments of **disappointment** too. When the kindergarten's building was damaged in a storm, she worried it would close. But parents raised money to **rebuild** it, and the school's **founding** director **publicly** thanked her for her help. In summer, she enjoys **sailing** on the lake near her home. \"I'm **glad** I found this job,\" she says. \"These children teach me something new every day.\""},"40":{"w":["bride","piano","angry","corner","dad","firmly","gym","mistaken","mouth","ours","sister","smoke","song","spoken","wake","winter","astronomer","autumn","breakthrough","brown"],"title":"A Winter Wedding","t":"Last **winter**, my older **sister** got married in a small hotel in the mountains. The **bride** wore a long white dress and a **brown** coat, and snow fell softly outside. Our **dad**, who rarely shows emotion, cried when he walked her down the aisle.\n\nThe wedding was simple but beautiful. A friend played the **piano**, and the guests sang my sister's favourite **song**. Her husband, an **astronomer**, gave a short speech about how they met while watching a meteor shower in **autumn**. \"It was a **breakthrough** moment in my life,\" he said, and everyone laughed.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. The hotel's heating stopped working, and the room filled with **smoke** from the old fireplace. A guest became **angry** and complained loudly. My sister just smiled. \"We are not here for perfection,\" she said **firmly**. \"We're here for love.\"\n\nThe next morning, I had to **wake** early to help clean up. In the **corner** of the room, I found a note in my sister's handwriting. It said: \"This day is **ours**, and no small problem will ruin it.\" I was **mistaken** to think she was nervous; she was calm all along. Later, she joked that she needed a week at the **gym** to recover from all the cake. I still remember her **spoken** vows, short and honest, and the way she laughed afterwards with her **mouth** full of cake."},"41":{"w":["businessman","click","database","dead","december","decoration","desk","friday","gardening","hometown","leg","loudly","september","shout","tall","unsuccessful","wash","wet","boat","bread"],"title":"Back to My Hometown","t":"Every **December**, I return to my **hometown**, a fishing village on the coast. This year, I arrived on a **Friday** evening in the rain. The streets were **wet**, and the wind was cold, but my mother was waiting at the bus stop with an umbrella and a bag of warm **bread**.\n\nThe village has changed a lot. When I left in **September** ten years ago, it was quiet and poor. Now there are new houses, a small hotel and a shop that sells Christmas **decorations|decoration** to tourists. A **businessman** from the city has opened a restaurant by the harbour, and young people use their phones to **click** and order food online.\n\nBut some things have not changed. My father still goes out in his small **boat** every morning, and he still comes home to **wash** the fish in the yard. He is **tall** and strong, though his **legs|leg** are not as fast as they once were. My mother spends her afternoons **gardening**, growing onions and herbs beside the house.\n\nOne evening, my father told me about his early life. His first business, a small fish shop, was **unsuccessful**, and he nearly gave up. \"I was so tired that I felt **dead**,\" he said. \"But your mother would **shout** at me to keep going.\" He laughed **loudly** at the memory. I wrote down his stories at the **desk** in my old bedroom, hoping to keep them in a family **database** for my children to read one day."},"42":{"w":["cat","evening","football","format","milk","railway","settler","sixth","adaptation","belly","birthday","blow","card","celebration","chopsticks","crossroads","cup","dance","dear","fluency"],"title":"A Birthday at the Crossroads","t":"My grandmother turned eighty last spring, and the whole family gathered for a **celebration** at her house near a busy **crossroads** in the old town. She has lived there for sixty years, since the **railway** first came to the area. \"I've watched this street change like a movie,\" she says.\n\nThe party began in the **evening**. My uncles brought food, my cousins decorated the walls, and the children made a **birthday** **card** with drawings of her favourite **cat**. We ate noodles for long life, and everyone used **chopsticks** except my cousin's husband, a Canadian who was still learning. \"It's an **adaptation**,\" he joked, holding his chopsticks like a pencil.\n\nAfter dinner, we watched a **football** match on television, and my grandmother cheered louder than anyone. Then she asked my sister to **dance** with her. Although her **belly** was full of cake, she danced happily for twenty minutes. My little brother tried to **blow** out the candles before she could, and everyone laughed.\n\nAt the end of the night, she made a short speech. \"My **dear** family,\" she began, \"this is my **sixth** decade in this house.\" She spoke with surprising **fluency**, though she was tired. Her own grandfather had been a **settler** from the north, she reminded us. She raised her **cup** of warm **milk** and thanked us all. My cousin later posted a video of the party, though it was in the wrong **format** and nobody could open it."},"43":{"w":["forty","grandparent","grass","happily","hurry","july","lend","mm","nationwide","parking","plane","pub","pupil","puzzled","regards","shall","shine","sincerely","sir","sunday"],"title":"A Letter to My Old Teacher","t":"Dear **Sir**,\n\nI hope you remember me. I was a **pupil** in your class more than **forty** years ago, in the small school by the river. I am writing on a rainy **Sunday** in **July**, at the same desk where I once did my homework. Outside, it has rained thirty **mm** in an hour, and the **grass** in the garden is shining.\n\nYou once told us that the sun **shall** **shine** again after every storm. I didn't understand you then, but I do now. When my business failed, and I felt completely **puzzled** about what to do, I remembered your words.\n\nI now work for a **nationwide** company and travel often by **plane**. Last month, I visited our old town. The school is gone, and there is a **parking** lot where the classroom used to be. The old **pub** opposite is still there, though, and the owner remembered you. He said you used to sit in the corner reading every afternoon.\n\nMy **grandparents|grandparent**, who lived next to the school, are no longer alive, but I often think of them. My grandmother used to **lend** me books when I was in a **hurry** to finish my homework. I **happily** remember those days.\n\nI would love to visit you, if you are well. Please give my **regards** to your family.\n\nYours **sincerely**,\nLi Ming"},"44":{"w":["swimming","tasty","twentieth","umbrella","wednesday","westerner","zero","banana","biography","dictionary","dinosaur","disobey","empty","encouragement","fairness","hobby","horse","pity","somebody","stamp"],"title":"My Little Brother's Hobbies","t":"My little brother is nine years old and has more **hobbies|hobby** than anyone I know. On **Wednesday** afternoons, he goes **swimming** at the local pool. On weekends, he rides a **horse** at a farm outside the city. And every evening, he reads a **biography** of a famous scientist or explorer.\n\nHis favourite subject is the **dinosaur**. He has a huge **dictionary** of prehistoric animals and can name more than a hundred species. Last month, he took second place in a school quiz, losing to a girl who answered every question correctly. \"It's a **pity**,\" he said, \"but she deserved to win. That's **fairness**.\"\n\nMy brother is generally well behaved, but he sometimes likes to **disobey** our parents. Once, he went out in the rain without an **umbrella**, and he came home soaking wet. Another time, he ate an entire bunch of **bananas|banana** and then complained of stomach ache.\n\nHe also collects old **stamps|stamp**. **Somebody** gave him a stamp printed in the early **twentieth** century, and he treats it like treasure. He keeps his collection in a box that was once **empty** but is now almost full. My parents give him a lot of **encouragement**, and he dreams of becoming a museum curator. When a **Westerner** visited our school, my brother asked him about stamps from his country. The visitor laughed and admitted that his own knowledge of stamps was close to **zero**. My mother made him a **tasty** cake to celebrate."},"45":{"w":["telephone","tennis","accountant","addicted","admirable","alcoholic","allergic","anecdote","aunt","basket","beer","beside","birthplace","bookstore","botany","bravery","brewery","brotherhood","brush","buffet"],"title":"My Aunt's Bookstore","t":"My **aunt** used to work as an **accountant** at a large company, but ten years ago she left to open a small **bookstore** in her **birthplace**, a quiet town in the south. It was a brave decision, and many people thought it would fail. But today, the shop is one of the most popular places in town.\n\nThe bookstore is full of character. Books are stacked in every corner, and a **basket** of free bookmarks sits **beside** the door. She sells books on everything from **botany** to cooking, and she likes to share an **anecdote** about every author. Visitors say her stories are as interesting as the books.\n\nOn weekends, she organises events. Once a month, she holds a reading evening with a **buffet** of homemade snacks. She serves tea and juice but no **alcoholic** drinks, because she wants families to feel welcome. Next door is a small **brewery**, and its owner sometimes sends over a bottle of **beer** for adult guests.\n\nMy aunt also teaches children to paint. She gives each child a **brush** and lets them decorate the shop windows. One boy was **allergic** to the paint, so she found a safer kind. \"Children become **addicted** to screens,\" she says, \"but painting gives them something real.\"\n\nThe people in the town admire her **bravery**. They say her shop has created a kind of **brotherhood** among readers. Many call her work **admirable**. She doesn't have a website, and she still takes orders by **telephone**. On Sundays, she plays **tennis** with friends in the park."},"46":{"w":["bureaucratic","bus","chick","choir","cinema","cookie","cuisine","deadline","delighted","disabled","disturbing","download","drier","eleven","except","explorer","fifteen","fifty","firewood","forgetful"],"title":"The Forgetful Explorer","t":"My uncle calls himself an **explorer**, though his adventures usually take place within **fifty** kilometres of home. He is famously **forgetful**: he loses his keys, his glasses and once even his car. But he is also the most cheerful person I know.\n\nLast winter, he invited me on a camping trip to the mountains. We took a **bus** to the last village and then walked for **fifteen** kilometres. My uncle carried the tent, while I carried **firewood** and food. Of course, he had forgotten the matches, **except** for one small box that I had packed by chance.\n\nThat night, we sat by the fire and talked. He told me about his work at a city office, which he described as slow and **bureaucratic**. \"Every **deadline** is a small nightmare,\" he said. He also told me some **disturbing** stories about a factory that once polluted a river near his childhood home.\n\nIn the morning, we visited a small farm where a **disabled** farmer raised **chicks|chick** and grew vegetables. He was **delighted** to have visitors and invited us to stay for lunch. His wife served local **cuisine** and homemade **cookies|cookie**. Their son sings in a church **choir** and dreams of studying music.\n\nWe returned home at **eleven** at night, tired but happy. My uncle immediately began to **download** his photos, while I used a hair **drier** to dry my wet socks. He says that next year we will go to the **cinema** instead. \"Less walking,\" he laughed."},"47":{"w":["furnished","gifted","grandma","ha","hair","handbag","hat","hey","hilly","homeland","housework","juicy","knife","lovely","memorize","messy","motorbike","motto","nose","novelist"],"title":"Grandma's Kitchen","t":"My **grandma** lives in a small, **hilly** village in the south, in a simple house **furnished** with old wooden chairs and a large table. Whenever I visit, she is in the kitchen, wearing a straw **hat** and an apron, chopping vegetables with an old **knife**.\n\nShe has a **gifted** hand for cooking. Her dumplings are famous in the village, and her fruit is always **juicy** and sweet. She never uses a recipe; she simply knows. \"Cooking is like singing,\" she says. \"You don't **memorize** it; you feel it.\"\n\nGrandma also has a **motto**: \"Work first, then rest.\" She does all the **housework** herself, though she is eighty years old. Her kitchen is never **messy**, and her grey **hair** is always neatly tied back. When I offer to help, she just says, \"**Ha**! You'll only get in the way.\"\n\nMy cousin, a **novelist** who lives in the city, often visits her for inspiration. He rides his **motorbike** from the city, carrying gifts in a small **handbag** for her. \"**Hey**, Grandma!\" he shouts as he arrives, and she always pretends to be surprised.\n\nShe loves her **homeland** deeply and says she will never move. \"The air here is clean,\" she says, touching her **nose**. \"I can smell the rain before it comes.\" When I leave, she gives me bags of vegetables. \"Come back soon,\" she says. \"It's **lovely** to have you here.\""},"48":{"w":["november","nursing","outing","passport","pen","pencil","performer","photographer","potato","receptionist","rice","shooting","sing","sixteen","smoking","snow","swap","taxi","teamwork","tenth"],"title":"The Photographer's Trip","t":"Last **November**, my friend Wen, a professional **photographer**, invited me on a work trip to a mountain village. She had been hired to take pictures for a travel magazine, and I agreed to help carry her equipment. We each packed a **passport** in case we crossed the border into Laos.\n\nOn the first day, we took a **taxi** from the airport to a small hotel. The **receptionist**, a cheerful young woman who was studying **nursing**, gave us a map and recommended a village **outing** the next morning. It was the **tenth** time Wen had visited the region, but she said she always found something new.\n\nThe village was beautiful. It began to **snow** lightly as we arrived, covering the rice fields in white. Families were cooking **rice** and **potato** soup over open fires. A local **performer** began to **sing** a traditional song, and children danced around him. Wen spent hours **shooting** photos of the scenes.\n\nLater, we sat with a group of villagers in a small house. A **sixteen**-year-old girl showed us her drawings, made with a single **pencil** and a cheap **pen**. Wen offered to **swap** one of her prints for a drawing, and the girl happily agreed. A sign on the wall said \"No **Smoking**,\" though the old men ignored it.\n\nWen says that good photography depends on **teamwork**. \"You can't do it alone,\" she told me. \"People must trust you.\""},"49":{"w":["theirs","thin","thriller","thursday","twenty","unconditional","underground","unimportant","vanilla","villager","workmate","yesterday","zoo","zoom","abortion","airmail","airspace","alley","announcement","anybody"],"title":"The Village Cinema","t":"Every **Thursday** evening, the **villagers|villager** of a small town in the north gather in an old warehouse for \"film night.\" A retired teacher started the event **twenty** years ago, using a projector he bought with his own money. It has become the most popular weekly event in town.\n\nThe films vary widely. Sometimes they watch an old comedy; at other times, a **thriller** or a documentary. Last month, they watched a foreign film about a family debate on **abortion**, which led to a serious but respectful discussion. \"Nothing is **unimportant** if it makes us think,\" the teacher says.\n\nThe warehouse is at the end of a narrow **alley**, beside an old **underground** car park. Inside, the seats are simple wooden benches, and a **thin** curtain hides the projector. Before each film, the teacher makes a short **announcement** about upcoming events. **Anybody** can come; entry is free.\n\nChildren love the evening. They eat **vanilla** ice cream and laugh loudly at cartoons about animals at the **zoo**. One boy likes to **zoom** in on pictures with his father's phone during breaks. His mother, a former **workmate** of the teacher, says the event gives the village a sense of community.\n\nThe teacher's love for film is **unconditional**. He still receives letters by **airmail** from a film club in Europe that sends him old movies. **Yesterday**, a plane flew low over the village, entering restricted **airspace**, and the noise interrupted the film. Everyone laughed. \"The seats are ours,\" the teacher said, \"but the sky is **theirs**.\""},"50":{"w":["apron","armchair","athletics","attentively","awesome","backache","bakery","ball","ballpoint","barbecue","barbershop","bathrobe","bathtub","battleground","beancurd","bedclothes","bedroom","beehive","bingo","biochemistry"],"title":"Saturday at Uncle Ma's House","t":"My uncle Ma is the most relaxed man I know. Every Saturday, he wakes up late in his **bedroom**, changes the **bedclothes**, puts on his **bathrobe** and sits in his old **armchair** with a cup of tea. \"Weekends are for rest,\" he says.\n\nAfter breakfast, he walks to the **bakery** on the corner to buy bread, then visits the **barbershop** next door for a haircut and a chat. The barber, an old friend, used to study **biochemistry** before he took over his father's shop. They argue about football and politics, and the shop becomes a friendly **battleground** of opinions.\n\nIn the afternoon, my uncle cooks. He wears a red **apron** and makes his famous fried **beancurd** with garlic. His garden has a small **beehive**, and he uses the honey in his sauces. In summer, he holds a **barbecue** for the neighbours, and the children play **ball** in the yard. \"This is **awesome**!\" my cousin always shouts.\n\nMy uncle once loved **athletics** and ran marathons, but now he suffers from **backache**. His doctor told him to relax in a warm **bathtub** every evening. He listens **attentively** to the doctor's advice, though he rarely follows it.\n\nOn Saturday evenings, he plays **bingo** with friends at the community centre. He keeps score with an old **ballpoint** pen that he has used for twenty years. \"Life is simple,\" he says. \"Enjoy the small things.\""},"51":{"w":["birdcage","blackboard","boating","bookcase","bookmark","bookshelf","bookshop","bored","botanical","bowling","boxing","breathless","brochure","brother","brunch","bun","bungalow","businesswoman","bye","cab"],"title":"A Weekend at the Lake","t":"Last month, my **brother** and I spent a weekend at a lake outside the city. We stayed in a small wooden **bungalow** owned by a friendly **businesswoman** who also ran a **bookshop** in town. Inside, every wall had a **bookshelf**, and a tall **bookcase** stood beside the fireplace.\n\nOn Saturday morning, we had **brunch** at a café by the water: coffee, eggs and a sweet **bun**. Then we went **boating** on the lake. My brother rowed so fast that he was soon **breathless**, and I laughed at him. In the afternoon, we visited a **botanical** garden, where we picked up a **brochure** about rare plants.\n\nMy brother is easily **bored**, so in the evening we went **bowling** in the nearby town. The bowling alley also had a small **boxing** club, and we watched a young boxer train. On the way back, we passed an old shop selling antiques, including an empty **birdcage** and an old school **blackboard**.\n\nBefore bed, I read a novel I had bought at the bookshop. I used a postcard as a **bookmark**. On Sunday, we took a **cab** to the train station. The owner of the bungalow waved and said, \"**Bye**! Come back soon!\" We promised we would."},"52":{"w":["caption","chairwoman","changeable","chant","clone","cloudy","comma","cooker","coral","cordless","courtyard","cowboy","crayon","cyclist","darkness","dirty","disgusting","doll","downtown","dumpling"],"title":"Growing Up Downtown","t":"I grew up in an old house with a **courtyard** in the **downtown** area of a busy city. My grandmother lived with us, and she made the best **dumplings|dumpling** in the neighbourhood. She cooked them on an old gas **cooker** that was older than my father.\n\nAs a child, I spent hours in the courtyard. I drew pictures with a **crayon** on the stone floor and played with my **doll**. On **cloudy** days, my grandmother told me stories about a **cowboy** who travelled across America, and I imagined galloping through the prairie. The weather was **changeable**, and we often ran inside when the rain began.\n\nOur street was always busy. Every morning, a **cyclist** delivered newspapers, and children would **chant** rhymes as they walked to school. Next door lived a woman who later became **chairwoman** of a local business association. She wrote **captions|caption** for photographs in the newspaper and taught me how to use a **comma** correctly.\n\nNot everything was pleasant. The alley behind our house was **dirty**, and in summer the smell from the rubbish bins was **disgusting**. At night, the **darkness** frightened me, and I kept a light on. My father bought us a **cordless** phone, the first in our street, which seemed like magic.\n\nToday, the old house is gone, replaced by a modern shopping centre. In its lobby there is an aquarium with **coral** and tropical fish. Sometimes I joke that the city has made a **clone** of every other city in the world, but my memories of that courtyard remain unique."},"53":{"w":["dustbin","dusty","dvd","ear","eastwards","eggplant","eighth","eraser","eyewitness","fingernail","flaming","flashlight","foggy","fourteen","franc","freeway","fried","gale","giraffe","glasshouse"],"title":"The Foggy Night","t":"It was the **eighth** of November, a cold and **foggy** night, when my car broke down on the **freeway** about **fourteen** kilometres from home. A strong **gale** was blowing, and I could barely see the road in front of me. I took out a **flashlight** and stepped outside.\n\nThe road was quiet. I walked **eastwards** along the shoulder, hoping to find help. My **ear** hurt from the cold wind, and my **fingernail** broke as I tried to open the bonnet. Finally, I saw lights from a small farm and knocked on the door.\n\nAn old farmer opened it. He invited me into his **dusty** kitchen, where a fire was **flaming** in the stove. His wife served me **fried** **eggplant** and rice. On the shelf were a **giraffe** statue, an old **DVD** player and a jar of foreign coins, including a French **franc** from his time as a sailor.\n\nThe farmer told me he had once been an **eyewitness** to a terrible accident on the same road. Since then, he always kept his lights on at night to help lost drivers. He also showed me his **glasshouse**, where he grew tomatoes all winter.\n\nIn the morning, his son towed my car to a garage. Before I left, I helped them carry rubbish to the **dustbin** outside and found a child's **eraser** on the ground. \"Our grandson's,\" the farmer laughed. \"He loses everything.\""},"54":{"w":["goldfish","goodbye","gram","grandchild","grandpa","granny","greengrocer","grill","gruel","gymnastics","handtruck","hardworking","headmistress","headteacher","hers","hibernate","hibernation","him","hive","hooray"],"title":"Visiting Grandpa and Granny","t":"Every winter holiday, I visit my **grandpa** and **granny** in their small village in the north. They have been married for sixty years, and I am their only **grandchild**. When I arrive, Granny shouts \"**Hooray**!\" and hugs me so tightly that I can hardly breathe.\n\nMy grandpa is a **hardworking** man who has been a farmer all his life. He used to be the village **headteacher** before he retired, and he still loves to teach. My granny was once **headmistress** of the girls' school in the next town. \"The books are **hers**,\" Grandpa says, pointing to a shelf. \"The tools are mine.\"\n\nIn winter, the village is quiet. Grandpa says that people **hibernate** like bears, staying indoors to keep warm. He explains how insects in their **hive** survive **hibernation** by staying close together. He keeps a bowl of **goldfish** by the window and feeds them every morning.\n\nGranny makes breakfast every day: a bowl of rice **gruel** and a few vegetables bought from the local **greengrocer**. She weighs each ingredient to the **gram**. On Sundays, Grandpa uses an old **grill** to cook meat, and I help him carry firewood with a **handtruck**. When I was young, I took **gymnastics** classes, and Granny still asks me to show her a cartwheel.\n\nWhen it is time to leave, Grandpa always walks me to the bus stop. \"Say **goodbye** to your parents for us,\" he says. I wave to **him** until the bus turns the corner."},"55":{"w":["hotdog","irrigate","jeep","jewelry","june","kangaroo","kilo","knock","labourer","league","leftover","lemonade","madame","mailbox","me","mend","minibus","miniskirt","motherland","motorcycle"],"title":"The Summer Market","t":"Every **June**, a small summer market opens in the square near my flat. It is one of my favourite events of the year. Farmers, craftsmen and food sellers set up stalls, and families come from all over the city. The **league** of local shopkeepers organises it, and the profits go to charity.\n\nThe market sells everything. One stall offers handmade **jewelry**, and another sells colourful **miniskirts|miniskirt** and scarves. A **labourer** who works in a nearby factory sells cakes made by his wife, and a farmer explains how he uses rainwater to **irrigate** his fields. A man in a **jeep** brings fresh fruit from the countryside, selling it by the **kilo**.\n\nChildren love the food. There are **hotdog** stands, ice cream and cold **lemonade**. My niece always asks **me** to buy her a toy **kangaroo** from a stall run by an Australian family. At the end of the day, sellers give their **leftover** food to the homeless shelter.\n\nThe organiser, an elegant French woman known as **Madame** Durand, has lived in China for twenty years. She calls China her second **motherland**. She drives a red **motorcycle** to the market every morning, and she helps sellers **mend** their tents when the wind damages them.\n\nOn the last evening, there is a concert. People arrive by **minibus** and sit on the grass. Before the music starts, my neighbour always comes to **knock** on my door to make sure I'm coming. A few days later, there is usually a thank-you card from Madame Durand in my **mailbox**."},"56":{"w":["moustache","necktie","ninety","ninth","noisily","noodle","noon","numb","offshore","oilfield","omelette","opener","ouch","outspoken","paddle","palace","pancake","paperwork","pardon","parrot"],"title":"The Oil Engineer","t":"My uncle worked for thirty years as an engineer on an **offshore** **oilfield** in the South China Sea. He spent weeks at a time on a platform far from land, surrounded only by water. \"At **noon**, the sun was so hot that my hands went **numb** from holding the hot rails,\" he remembers. \"At night, the wind was so strong that the platform shook.\"\n\nLife on the platform was simple. The workers ate **noodles|noodle**, eggs and the occasional **omelette**. My uncle kept a small **parrot** in his room, which learned to say \"**Pardon**?\" whenever someone spoke. The other workers loved it, and it became the team's lucky charm.\n\nMy uncle is **outspoken** and never afraid to say what he thinks. He once complained to the company about the endless **paperwork**, which he said took more time than real work. His manager, a strict man with a thick **moustache** and a bright **necktie**, laughed and agreed.\n\nNow he is retired and lives near the sea. He goes out in a small boat and likes to **paddle** along the coast. On Sundays, he makes **pancakes|pancake** for his grandchildren and tells them stories about the platform. \"**Ouch**!\" he shouted last week, when he burned his finger on the pan. The children laughed **noisily**.\n\nLast spring, he visited the old **palace** in Beijing for the **ninth** time. He plans to live to **ninety**, and he jokes that the secret is a cold drink every evening, which is why his bottle **opener** is the most important tool in his kitchen."},"57":{"w":["pence","pianist","playmate","playroom","pleased","policeman","popcorn","pork","postbox","postcard","postcode","preview","printing","punctuate","punctuation","pyramid","quake","raincoat","rainfall","receipt"],"title":"The Old Post Office","t":"In the centre of my hometown stands an old post office with a red **postbox** outside. When I was a child, I went there every week with my grandmother to buy stamps and send a **postcard** to my aunt in England. The clerk, a kind woman, always gave me a sweet and said she was **pleased** to see me.\n\nThe post office was also the town's meeting place. A **policeman** often stopped there for tea, and an old **pianist** played in the café next door. My **playmate** and I sat in a small **playroom** at the back while the adults talked. We ate **popcorn** and read comics.\n\nToday, the post office has changed. People now type a **postcode** into their phones instead of writing letters, and the old **printing** machine has been replaced by computers. But the building has been kept as a museum. Visitors can **preview** an exhibition of old letters online before they visit.\n\nOne room shows letters written during the great **quake** of 1976, when thousands of people wrote to their families. A schoolteacher explains how the letters were written in a hurry, with little **punctuation**. \"People didn't **punctuate** their sentences,\" she says, \"because every second mattered.\"\n\nThe museum café sells **pork** buns and tea. For fun, the menu also shows the prices in British **pence**, just as my aunt's old letters from England did. Outside, I watched a man in a yellow **raincoat** checking the **rainfall** gauge. When I paid for my tea, the waiter handed me a **receipt** shaped like a stamp, and a small paper **pyramid** for my table number."},"58":{"w":["redirect","reuse","reviewer","rewind","rewrite","rhyme","rugby","salesgirl","saleswoman","salty","saturday","schoolbag","schoolmate","seagull","seashell","seaweed","secondhand","semicircle","seventh","sharpener"],"title":"By the Sea","t":"On the **seventh** day of the summer holiday, my **schoolmate** Lina and I took a bus to the sea. It was a sunny **Saturday**, and the beach was full of families. We carried our snacks in a **schoolbag** and found a quiet place near the rocks.\n\nWe spent the morning collecting **seashells|seashell** and watching **seagulls|seagull** fly over the water. The air was **salty**, and the rocks were covered in green **seaweed**. Lina drew a **semicircle** in the sand and asked me to make a sandcastle inside it.\n\nIn the afternoon, we watched a game of **rugby** on the beach. A **saleswoman** walked along the sand selling ice cream, and a young **salesgirl** sold cheap **secondhand** sunglasses. I bought a pair for my little brother.\n\nLina wants to be a writer. She brought a notebook and a pencil **sharpener**, and she wrote a short poem about the sea. \"Does it **rhyme**?\" I asked. \"Not yet,\" she said. \"I'll **rewrite** it tonight.\" She likes to **rewind** her favourite films to study how characters talk. Her teacher, a strict **reviewer**, always tells her to **redirect** her energy into reading.\n\nBefore we left, we collected our rubbish so that we could **reuse** the bags. The sun was setting, and the sea looked like gold."},"59":{"w":["shaver","sheep","shirt","shorts","sideway","sixteenth","skateboard","skillfully","skirt","smelly","smoker","sneaker","snowball","snowman","socket","softball","spoonful","squid","stairs","stomachache"],"title":"Winter Fun","t":"When I was a child, winter was my favourite season. As soon as the first snow fell, my brother and I would rush outside in our boots and coats to build a **snowman**. We gave him a carrot nose and a red scarf, and we always started a **snowball** fight afterwards.\n\nMy mother insisted we dress warmly. She made me wear a thick **shirt** under my coat and long trousers instead of **shorts**. My sister, who loved fashion, wanted to wear a **skirt** in the snow, but my mother refused. \"You'll get a **stomachache** from the cold,\" she warned.\n\nOur neighbour, an old farmer, kept **sheep** in a field behind our house. In winter, he carried hay to them every morning. He was a heavy **smoker**, and his clothes were always **smelly**, but he was kind. He taught my brother to ride a **skateboard** along the frozen path, moving **skillfully** around the ice.\n\nAt lunch, my mother made **squid** soup and gave each of us a **spoonful** of honey. In the evening, my father plugged the heater into the old **socket** in the living room. We climbed the **stairs** to bed, tired and happy.\n\nOn the **sixteenth** of January, my birthday, my parents gave me a pair of new **sneakers|sneaker** and a **softball**. My father, who had just received a new electric **shaver**, looked unusually smart that day. We walked to the park by a narrow **sideway** behind the houses, throwing snow at each other all the way."},"60":{"w":["stopwatch","subtraction","suite","sunburnt","sunglasses","sunny","superman","supper","swim","tasteless","teapot","thankful","them","thermos","thirsty","thirteen","thunderstorm","till","toothache","toothbrush"],"title":"A Summer Picnic","t":"Last summer, my family went on a picnic by a lake. It was a **sunny** day, and we were all **thirsty** after the long walk from the car park. My mother had packed a **thermos** of iced tea, a **teapot** for later and enough sandwiches for an army.\n\nMy little brother, who is **thirteen**, wanted to **swim** in the lake immediately. He wore his **Superman** T-shirt and a pair of **sunglasses**. My father brought a **stopwatch** to time his races. \"Ten minutes!\" my brother shouted proudly. My father pretended to do a quick **subtraction**: \"Then you've got five minutes left before lunch.\"\n\nAfter lunch, the sky grew dark, and a **thunderstorm** began. We ran to a small café and waited **till** the rain stopped. The café served a **tasteless** cake, but we were **thankful** for the shelter. My mother complained that her shoulders were **sunburnt**.\n\nThat evening, we stayed in a hotel **suite** near the lake. We ate a simple **supper** of noodles and vegetables. My brother had a **toothache**, so my mother gave him a new **toothbrush** and told him to brush gently.\n\nBefore bed, my father told us a story. \"Was it a good day?\" he asked. \"Yes,\" we told **them**. \"The best.\""},"61":{"w":["toothpaste","tournament","translator","twelfth","twelve","unbelievable","uncle","undivided","unmarried","unrest","untrue","us","veal","videophone","violinist","walkman","washroom","watermelon","wayside","weatherman"],"title":"My Uncle the Translator","t":"My **uncle** is a **translator** who works for an international company. He speaks five languages and travels around the world. Last year, he invited my brother and me to visit him in Spain, where he was working at a tennis **tournament**.\n\nThe trip was **unbelievable**. He showed **us** the city, took us to restaurants and introduced us to his friends. One of them was a **violinist** who played in the city orchestra; another was a **weatherman** on local television. My uncle is **unmarried**, and he says his work is his family.\n\nHe told us stories about his career. Once, during a period of political **unrest** in a foreign country, he had to translate for journalists. Some newspapers published **untrue** reports, and he worked hard to correct them. \"A translator must give **undivided** attention to every word,\" he said.\n\nIn Spain, we ate grilled **veal** and cold **watermelon**. My uncle laughed at my old **walkman**, which I had brought to listen to music. \"That's a museum piece,\" he said. He still remembers using an early **videophone** in the 1990s.\n\nOn the **twelfth** day, we drove into the countryside and stopped at a **wayside** café. The **washroom** was tiny, and I forgot my **toothpaste** at the hotel. We stayed for **twelve** days in total, and it was the best holiday of my life."},"62":{"w":["westwards","willingly","windbreaker","woo","worn","wounded","yourselves","yummy","zip","zipper"],"title":"Heading West","t":"Last autumn, my friends and I drove **westwards** across the desert. We packed warm jackets, and I wore my old **windbreaker**, **worn** at the elbows but still warm. My friend's bag broke on the first day because the **zipper** stuck, and we had to **zip** it closed with tape.\n\nOn the way, we helped a **wounded** dog by the roadside and took it to a vet. My friends **willingly** shared their food with it. At night, we cooked noodles over a fire. \"**Yummy**!\" said my friend, though the noodles were half-cooked. The trip helped me **woo** back my love of travel. \"Enjoy **yourselves**,\" the vet had told us, and we did."}});
